# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#  Copy this to `.env.docker` at the repository root:
#
#      cp docker/env.example .env.docker
#
#  Then fill in AUTH_SECRET and the two passwords. `.env.docker` is git-ignored
#  (the root .gitignore ignores every `.env*`); this file is the committed
#  template, so it must never hold a real secret.
#
#  Note there is no DATABASE_URL here. docker-compose.yml builds it from the
#  MYSQL_* values below and sets it on each service, so the app can only ever
#  point at the database beside it — not at the remote server in your `.env`.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Signs the session cookie and the native app's bearer tokens — one secret,
# both paths. Changing it signs everybody out. Generate one with:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
AUTH_SECRET=""

# The MySQL passwords. Compose interpolates these into a URL, so keep them to
# letters and digits: a `@`, `:`, `/` or `#` would need percent-encoding and
# will otherwise silently truncate the connection string. Generate one with:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(18).toString('hex'))"
MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=""
MYSQL_PASSWORD=""

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The account the app connects as, and the database it uses. The defaults are
# fine; they are only reachable from inside the compose network.
# MYSQL_USER="mizmar"
# MYSQL_DATABASE="mizmar_school"

# What the `migrate` service does after applying migrations. All four are safe
# to leave in place — the seeds are idempotent, so re-running one on every
# `docker compose up` changes nothing.
#
#   config  years, cursus, rooms, fees, roles, one administrator  (default)
#   demo    all of that plus pupils, staff, classes, receipts     (slow)
#   empty   an organisation and nothing else
#   none    apply migrations only
#
# Worth setting to `none` once the school has real data in it, purely to save
# the few seconds it costs on every restart.
# SEED_MODE="config"

# The first administrator, written by the seed. Set these BEFORE the first
# `docker compose up`: the seeds upsert on the username derived from the email,
# so changing it later adds a second administrator rather than renaming the
# first. Everybody signs in with a username — `admin@groupescolaire.ma` means
# signing in as `admin`, and the address itself is never a credential.
# SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL="admin@groupescolaire.ma"
# SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD="Admin123!"

# The organisation's name, for SEED_MODE=empty.
# SEED_ORG_NAME="Votre établissement"

# The port the app is published on, on the host.
# APP_PORT="3000"

# The port phpMyAdmin is published on, on the host. It only runs when you ask
# for it: `docker compose --profile tools up -d phpmyadmin`.
# PMA_PORT_HOST="8080"

# ── WhatsApp reminders (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave all of this out and the app simply has no messaging: no sender runs.
# To turn it on, fill these and start with `--profile whatsapp`:
#
#     docker compose --profile whatsapp up -d --build
#
# Then open http://127.0.0.1:2785 on the server, create a session named as
# OPENWA_SESSION below, and scan the QR with the school's DEDICATED number.
# WhatsApp can restrict accounts driven this way; never use a personal number.
#
# The key both sides share. Generate one with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(24).toString('hex'))"
# OPENWA_API_KEY=""
# API_MASTER_KEY=""     # the same value again: this is the name OpenWA reads
# OPENWA_URL="http://openwa:2785"
# OPENWA_SESSION="school"
#
# Milliseconds between two messages (a random half-again is added). Slow is the
# safety measure; do not shorten it to hurry a batch.
# MESSAGING_DELAY_MS="6000"
#
# Who may add message credits: comma-separated usernames of super admins. Empty
# means any super admin. Set it to YOUR username on a school's deployment.
# MESSAGING_OWNER_USERNAMES=""
